    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d302">

                <pb facs="0389" n="281" sameAs="frus:frus1927v02/pg281"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.11/551: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-04-06T06:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 6, 1927—6
                                a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received April 5—6:40
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>357. Following from American consul at Hankow: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“April 4, 5 p.m. Twelve Americans departed via <hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Woosung</hi> yesterday morning; 9 by the <hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Kian</hi> and 25 via <hi rendition="#italic">Woon Ho</hi> last
                            night. There remain here 89 men, 19 women and 12 children. Nearly all
                            these are sleeping aboard ships. Additional departures tomorrow or next
                            day. Admiral Hough<anchor n="16" xml:id="d302-f1"/> returned here this
                            morning.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d302-f1" n="16" xml:id="d302-f1-content">
                <p>Rear Admiral Henry H. Hough, U. S. N., commander of the Yangtze Patrol.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
